/F1 8.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 806 Tm (ECON 101 Principles of Economics, Chapter 3) Tj
/F2 20 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 768 Tm (Chapter 3: Supply and Demand) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 733 Tm (A market is a group of buyers and sellers of a particular good or service. The buyers as a) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 717.5 Tm (group determine the demand for the product, and the sellers as a group determine the supply) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 702.0 Tm (of the product. In a competitive market, there are so many buyers and sellers that each has) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 686.5 Tm (a negligible impact on the market price.) Tj
/F2 13.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 658.0 Tm (Demand) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 635.0 Tm (The law of demand states that, other things equal, the quantity demanded of a good falls) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 619.5 Tm (when the price of the good rises. The demand curve is a graph of the relationship between) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 604.0 Tm (the price of a good and the quantity demanded. Because a lower price increases the quantity) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 588.5 Tm (demanded, the demand curve slopes downward.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 568.0 Tm (Income also affects demand. Normal goods are goods for which demand rises when income rises.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 552.5 Tm (Inferior goods are goods for which demand falls when income rises; bus rides are a common) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 537.0 Tm (example.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 516.5 Tm (Substitutes are pairs of goods for which an increase in the price of one leads to an) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 501.0 Tm (increase in the demand for the other. Complements are pairs of goods for which an increase) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 485.5 Tm (in the price of one leads to a decrease in the demand for the other.) Tj
/F1 8.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 280 30 Tm (Page 1 of 3) Tj
