/F1 8.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 806 Tm (ECON 101 Principles of Economics, Chapter 3) Tj
/F2 13.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 760 Tm (Supply) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 737 Tm (The law of supply states that, other things equal, the quantity supplied of a good rises) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 721.5 Tm (when the price of the good rises. The supply curve slopes upward because a higher price) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 706.0 Tm (makes production more profitable.) Tj
/F2 13.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 677.5 Tm (Equilibrium) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 654.5 Tm (Equilibrium is a situation in which the market price has reached the level at which quantity) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 639.0 Tm (supplied equals quantity demanded. A surplus is a situation in which quantity supplied is) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 623.5 Tm (greater than quantity demanded. A shortage is a situation in which quantity demanded is) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 608.0 Tm (greater than quantity supplied.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 587.5 Tm (When there is a surplus, sellers lower their prices, which moves the market toward) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 572.0 Tm (equilibrium.) Tj
/F2 13.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 543.5 Tm (Elasticity) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 520.5 Tm (Price elasticity of demand measures how much the quantity demanded responds to a change in) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 505.0 Tm (price. Demand is elastic when the elasticity is greater than 1 and inelastic when it is less) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 489.5 Tm (than 1. Necessities tend to have inelastic demand, while luxuries tend to have elastic) Tj
/F1 11 Tf 1 0 0 1 56 474.0 Tm (demand.) Tj
/F1 8.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 280 30 Tm (Page 2 of 3) Tj
